+After the initial migration, the reviewed Stage 1 design gate (`docs/stage1/revised-physiological-architecture.md`, supported by `docs/stage1/literature-and-evidence-matrix.md` and the preserved `docs/stage1/baseline-scientific-report.md`) replaced the three-compartment, single-sympathetic-state core with the Stage 1 four-compartment, eleven-state model (separate splanchnic and lower-limb venous compartments; cardiac-sympathetic, vascular-sympathetic, and vagal effector states; an explicit rhythmic muscle pump; an independent sustained-contraction impediment; and a cerebral-perfusion-pressure-based flow index). The validation consequences of that historical revision are:
 
 - Conservation tests now cover all four volumes, including muscle-pump transfer, at both the RHS-telescoping and integrated-drift levels.
 - Every one of the ten registered configurations is tested as an exact supine fixed point (maximum absolute RHS component below 1e-9); uniform volume scaling alone does not preserve the fixed point, so per-phenotype resistance and setpoint derivations are required and tested.
 - The orthostatic scenario criterion is the sustained final-120-second-upright HR rise; the peak rise is a separate secondary metric.
